T*()Tj
T*(In the)Tj
T*(United States Court of Appeals)Tj
T*(For the Seventh Circuit)Tj
T*()Tj
T*(No. 99-1736)Tj
T*()Tj
T*(JAMES W. ADAIR,)Tj
T*()Tj
T*(Plaintiff-Appellant,)Tj
T*()Tj
T*(v. )Tj
T*()Tj
T*(MICHAEL L. SHERMAN and)Tj
T*(SHERMAN & SHERMAN,)Tj
T*()Tj
T*(Defendants-Appellees.)Tj
T*()Tj
T*()Tj
T*()Tj
T*(Appeal from the United States District Court)Tj
T*(for the Northern District of Illinois, Eastern Division.)Tj
T*(No. 98 C 3946--George M. Marovich, Judge.)Tj
T*()Tj
T*()Tj
T*(Argued February 9, 2000--Decided August 25, 2000)Tj
T*()Tj
T*()Tj
T*()Tj
T*(  Before BAUER, EASTERBROOK and RIPPLE, Circuit Judges.)Tj
T*()Tj
T*(  RIPPLE, Circuit Judge.  James Adair brought this)Tj
T*(action under the Fair Debt Collection Practices)Tj
T*(Act \("FDCPA"\), 15 U.S.C. sec. 1692 et seq. He)Tj
T*(contends that Michael Sherman and his law firm,)Tj
T*(Sherman & Sherman \(collectively "Sherman &)Tj
T*(Sherman"\), overvalued their secured claims in Mr.)Tj
T*(Adair's Chapter 13 bankruptcy proceedings. The)Tj
T*(district court held that Mr. Adair's action was)Tj
T*(barred because he had failed to object to the)Tj
T*(valuation of the claim in the bankruptcy court.)Tj
T*(For the reasons set forth in the following)Tj
T*(opinion, we affirm the judgment of the district)Tj
T*(court.)Tj
T*()Tj
T*(I)Tj
T*(BACKGROUND)Tj
T*()Tj
T*(  Because the district court dismissed Mr. Adair's)Tj
T*(complaint for failing to state a claim, we)Tj
T*(consider all facts in the light most favorable to)Tj
T*(him. See Hernandez v. Joliet Police Dept., 197)Tj
T*(F.3d 256, 262 \(7th Cir. 1999\). In March 1997, Mr.)Tj
T*(Adair obtained a loan from First Midwest Bank)Tj
T*(\("FMB"\), with an amount financed of $16,483.22.)Tj
T*(The loan was secured by a 1995 Chevrolet Lumina)Tj
T*(automobile. In July, Mr. Adair filed for)Tj
T*(bankruptcy under Chapter 13. His bankruptcy plan)Tj
T*(provided that all allowed secured claims would be)Tj
T*(paid in full, with unsecured creditors to receive)Tj
ET

endstream 
