/F1 10 Tf
1 0 0 1 50 752 Tm
12 TL
T*(a percentage of their allowed claims.)Tj
T*()Tj
T*(  On September 3, Sherman & Sherman filed a proof)Tj
T*(of claim on behalf of FMB. The proof of claim)Tj
T*(listed the value of the Chevrolet as $19,841.43,)Tj
T*(an amount greater than the car's original)Tj
T*(purchase price. Mr. Adair did not object to the)Tj
T*(valuation of the car prior to confirmation. The)Tj
T*(Chapter 13 trustee confirmed Mr. Adair's)Tj
T*(bankruptcy plan on September 15, and allowed)Tj
T*(FMB's claim as fully secured. In June 1998, Mr.)Tj
T*(Adair filed an adversary proceeding in the)Tj
T*(bankruptcy court challenging FMB's proof of)Tj
T*(claim; that proceeding was dismissed when Mr.)Tj
T*(Adair's Chapter 13 proceeding was dismissed)Tj
T*(altogether./1)Tj
T*()Tj
T*(  Mr. Adair subsequently filed this FDCPA)Tj
T*(complaint in district court, seeking damages for)Tj
T*(what he alleged was Sherman & Sherman's practice)Tj
T*(of overvaluing collateral in proofs of claims)Tj
T*(filed with the bankruptcy court. He contends that)Tj
T*(Sherman & Sherman overvalued collateral)Tj
T*(fraudulently, in order to establish as secured)Tj
T*(claims that should have been unsecured. The)Tj
T*(district court granted Sherman & Sherman's motion)Tj
T*(to dismiss and held that the action was barred by)Tj
T*(claim preclusion, also known as res judicata.)Tj
T*()Tj
T*(II)Tj
T*(DISCUSSION)Tj
T*()Tj
T*(  Although the district court articulated its)Tj
T*(decision in terms of claim preclusion, we believe)Tj
T*(that this case is more appropriately analyzed)Tj
T*(under the closely related, although analytically)Tj
T*(distinct, doctrine of collateral estoppel or)Tj
T*(issue preclusion./2 Under the doctrine of issue)Tj
T*(preclusion, an issue may not be litigated if the)Tj
T*(following conditions are met: \(1\) the issue)Tj
T*(sought to be precluded is the same as that)Tj
T*(involved in a prior action; \(2\) the issue was)Tj
T*(actually relitigated; \(3\) the determination of)Tj
T*(the issue was essential to the final judgment;)Tj
T*(and \(4\) the party against whom estoppel is)Tj
T*(invoked was represented in the prior action. See)Tj
T*(Chicago Truck Drivers, Helpers & Warehouse Union)Tj
T*(\(Indep.\) Pension Fund v. Century Motor Freight,)Tj
T*(Inc., 125 F.3d 526, 530 \(7th Cir. 1997\); La)Tj
T*(Preferida, Inc. v. Cerveceria Mondelo, 914 F.2d)Tj
T*(900, 905-06 \(7th Cir. 1990\). As the Supreme Court)Tj
T*(of the United States has stated: "Under)Tj
T*(collateral estoppel, once an issue is actually)Tj
T*(and necessarily determined by a court of)Tj
T*(competent jurisdiction, that determination is)Tj
T*(conclusive in subsequent suits based on a)Tj
T*(different cause of action involving a party to)Tj
T*(the prior litigation." Montana, 440 U.S. at 153.)Tj
T*("Whether the issues are identical is a question)Tj
T*(of law." E.B. Harper & Co. v. Nortek, Inc., 104)Tj
ET

