T*(F.3d 913, 922 \(7th Cir. 1997\). We decide such)Tj
T*(questions de novo. See Caruso v. De Luca, 81 F.3d)Tj
T*(666, 670 \(7th Cir. 1996\).)Tj
T*()Tj
T*(  Issue preclusion is an affirmative defense. See)Tj
T*(Blonder-Tongue Lab., Inc. v. University of Ill.)Tj
T*(Found., 402 U.S. 313, 350 \(1971\). Therefore, the)Tj
T*(defendant has the burden to set forth facts)Tj
T*(sufficient to satisfy each element of the)Tj
T*(defense. See Havoco of Am., Ltd. v. Freeman,)Tj
T*(Atkins & Coleman, Ltd., 58 F.3d 303, 306 \(7th)Tj
T*(Cir. 1995\); La Preferida, 914 F.2d at 906. We now)Tj
T*(consider whether Sherman & Sherman has satisfied)Tj
T*(each of these requirements. As we pointed out in)Tj
T*(Havoco, in determining whether these criteria)Tj
T*(have been met, it is often necessary for the)Tj
T*(court to consult substantive principles of law in)Tj
T*(order to determine the scope of the earlier)Tj
T*(judicial determination or the parties bound by)Tj
T*(that determination. See 58 F.3d at 308. In the)Tj
T*(case before us, it will be necessary to refer to)Tj
T*(the substantive law of bankruptcy and, indeed, to)Tj
T*(some extent, to the FDCPA, to determine whether)Tj
T*(the principles of issue preclusion ought to)Tj
T*(apply.)Tj
T*()Tj
T*(A.)Tj
T*()Tj
T*(  We begin by examining the action of the)Tj
T*(bankruptcy court in order to determine the nature)Tj
T*(and the scope of its determination. We must)Tj
T*(ascertain whether the bankruptcy court actually)Tj
T*(and necessarily decided an issue that would now)Tj
T*(preclude recovery in the FDCPA action. Sherman &)Tj
T*(Sherman filed a proof of claim listing the value)Tj
T*(of the 1995 Chevrolet as $19,841.43. According to)Tj
T*(the bankruptcy code, any proof of claim filed by)Tj
T*(a creditor is deemed allowed, unless a party in)Tj
T*(interest objects. See 11 U.S.C. sec. 502\(a\); In)Tj
T*(re Greenig, 152 F.3d 631, 633 \(7th Cir. 1998\)./3)Tj
T*("A proof of claim executed and filed in)Tj
T*(accordance with the Bankruptcy Rules constitutes)Tj
T*(prima facie evidence of the validity and amount)Tj
T*(of the claim." In re Ross, 162 B.R. 785, 788)Tj
T*(\(Bankr. N.D. Ill. 1993\) \(citing Fed. R. Bankr. P.)Tj
T*(3001\(f\)\).)Tj
T*()Tj
T*(  Mr. Adair had notice of the proof of claim prior)Tj
T*(to confirmation, but he chose not to object to)Tj
T*(it. "As a general rule, the failure to raise an)Tj
T*(objection at the confirmation hearing or to)Tj
T*(appeal from the order of confirmation should)Tj
T*(preclude attack on the plan or any provision)Tj
T*(therein as illegal in a subsequent proceeding.")Tj
T*(In re Chappell, 984 F.2d 775, 782 \(7th Cir. 1993\))Tj
T*(\(quotation marks and ellipses omitted\); see also)Tj
T*(In re Pence, 905 F.2d 1107, 1110 \(7th Cir. 1990\).)Tj
T*(In our decision in Pence, we refused relief to a)Tj
T*(creditor who, "instead of attacking the valuation)Tj
T*(head-on at the confirmation hearing," chose "a)Tj
ET

endstream 
