1 0 0 1 50 752 Tm
12 TL
T*(collateral attack on the confirmation order where)Tj
T*(valuation may not be contested." Id. at 1110. Our)Tj
T*(sister circuits share our view that once a)Tj
T*(bankruptcy plan is confirmed, its terms are not)Tj
T*(subject to collateral attack. See Andersen v.)Tj
T*(UNIPAC-NEBHELP, 179 F.3d 1253, 1258-59 \(10th Cir.)Tj
T*(1999\); In re Varat Enters., Inc., 81 F.3d 1310,)Tj
T*(1315-17 \(4th Cir. 1996\); In re Justice Oaks II,)Tj
T*(Ltd., 898 F.2d 1544, 1553 \(11th Cir.\), cert.)Tj
T*(denied, 498 U.S. 959 \(1990\); In re Szostek, 886)Tj
T*(F.2d 1405, 1413 \(3d Cir. 1989\); In re Gregory,)Tj
T*(705 F.2d 1118, 1121 \(9th Cir. 1983\). )Tj
T*()Tj
T*(  These authorities lead us to the conclusion)Tj
T*(that, when a proof of claim is filed prior to)Tj
T*(confirmation,/4 and the debtor does not object)Tj
T*(prior to confirmation,/5 the debtor may not file)Tj
T*(a post-confirmation collateral action that calls)Tj
T*(into question the proof of claim. See Justice)Tj
T*(Oaks, 898 F.2d at 1553; Ross, 162 B.R. at 789)Tj
T*(\("The law is well settled that a confirmation)Tj
T*(order is res judicata as to all issues decided or)Tj
T*(which could have been decided at the hearing on)Tj
T*(confirmation."\)./6 Allowing collateral attacks)Tj
T*(of the type brought by Mr. Adair would give)Tj
T*(debtors an incentive to refrain from objecting in)Tj
T*(the bankruptcy proceeding and would thereby)Tj
T*(destroy the finality that bankruptcy confirmation)Tj
T*(is intended to provide./7)Tj
T*()Tj
T*(  In short, the bankruptcy process provides)Tj
T*(protection against fraudulent proofs of claims.)Tj
T*(Mr. Adair had the opportunity to contest Sherman)Tj
T*(& Sherman's proof of claim and practices related)Tj
T*(thereto in the bankruptcy court. Because he chose)Tj
T*(not to, he is barred from doing so here./8)Tj
T*(B.)Tj
T*()Tj
T*(  We are also convinced that issue preclusion)Tj
T*(applies because the FDCPA is an improper vehicle)Tj
T*(for challenging the amount of a debt established)Tj
T*(by the bankruptcy court. The FDCPA regulates the)Tj
T*(practices used to collect a debt./9 See Keele v.)Tj
T*(Wexler, 149 F.3d 589, 594 \(7th Cir. 1998\) \("[T]he)Tj
T*(FDCPA is designed to protect consumers from the)Tj
T*(unscrupulous antics of debt collectors,)Tj
T*(irrespective of whether a valid debt actually)Tj
T*(exists."\); see also Hawthorne v. Mac Adjustment,)Tj
T*(Inc., 140 F.3d 1367, 1370-71 \(11th Cir. 1998\);)Tj
T*(Mabe v. G.C. Servs. Ltd. Partnership, 32 F.3d 86,)Tj
T*(87-88 \(4th Cir. 1994\); Zimmerman v. HBO Affiliate)Tj
T*(Group, 834 F.2d 1163, 1167 \(3d Cir. 1987\). As Mr.)Tj
T*(Adair has framed his FDCPA action, it cannot)Tj
T*(succeed without a showing that the existence or)Tj
T*(amount of his debt was established improperly.)Tj
T*(However, this circuit and other federal courts)Tj
T*(have held that an FDCPA action is not an action)Tj
T*(to establish a debt but an action contesting the)Tj
T*(method of collection of that debt. See Whitaker)Tj
T*(v. Ameritech Corp., 129 F.3d 952, 957-58 \(7th)Tj
ET

endstream 
