T*()Tj
T*(/3 Parties in interest include not only the debtor,)Tj
T*(but anyone who has a legally protected interest)Tj
T*(that could be affected by a bankruptcy)Tj
T*(proceeding. See In re FBN Food Servs., Inc., 82)Tj
T*(F.3d 1387, 1391 \(7th Cir. 1996\). Therefore, if)Tj
T*(one creditor files a potentially fraudulent proof)Tj
T*(of claim, other creditors have standing to object)Tj
T*(to the proof of claim.)Tj
T*()Tj
T*(/4 We need not address the practice in bankruptcy)Tj
T*(courts of allowing proofs of claims to be filed)Tj
T*(after confirmation. See, e.g., In re Witkowski,)Tj
T*(16 F.3d 739, 741 \(7th Cir. 1994\); In re Strong,)Tj
T*(203 B.R. 105, 114 \(Bankr. N.D. Ill. 1996\). We)Tj
T*(address only the situation in which the creditor)Tj
T*(filed a proof of claim before confirmation and)Tj
T*(the debtor had enough time to formulate an)Tj
T*(objection prior to confirmation. )Tj
T*()Tj
T*(/5 Mr. Adair has not argued that the time between)Tj
T*(the filing of Sherman & Sherman's proof of claim)Tj
T*(and the confirmation hearing was insufficient to)Tj
T*(allow him to prepare an objection.)Tj
T*(/6 There has been some tension in bankruptcy court)Tj
T*(cases as to whether debtors are required to)Tj
T*(object to proofs of claims prior to confirmation.)Tj
T*(See In re Simmons, 224 B.R. 879, 883-84 \(Bankr.)Tj
T*(N.D. Ill. 1998\) \(noting cases\). We respectfully)Tj
T*(choose not to follow those cases allowing post-)Tj
T*(confirmation objections to proofs of claims to be)Tj
T*(filed even though the proof of claim itself was)Tj
T*(filed sufficiently in advance of the confirmation)Tj
T*(hearing. See In re Church, No. 96 B 18347, 1998)Tj
T*(WL 97691, at *3-*4 \(Bankr. N.D. Ill. Mar. 2,)Tj
T*(1998\).)Tj
T*()Tj
T*(/7 We note the thoughtful analysis of a different)Tj
T*(district court in an action similar to the one)Tj
T*(before us:)Tj
T*()Tj
T*(Plaintiff's claims . . . raise the serious)Tj
T*(concern that bankruptcy debtors will deliberately)Tj
T*(fail to challenge proofs of claim, despite having)Tj
T*(knowledge of possible challenges to the proofs of)Tj
T*(claim and despite being represented in their)Tj
T*(bankruptcy, in the hope of maintaining collateral)Tj
T*(challenges pursuant to statutes, like the FDCPA,)Tj
T*(which may provide additional damages, as well as)Tj
T*(attorney's fees. If debtors were permitted to)Tj
T*(make such strategic decisions and, thus, to delay)Tj
T*(their challenges to the legality or correctness)Tj
T*(of proofs of claim until after dismissal of the)Tj
T*(bankruptcy case, the concept of finality in)Tj
T*(bankruptcy will be completely undermined.)Tj
T*()Tj
T*(Baldwin v. McCalla, Raymer, Padrick, Cobb,)Tj
T*(Nichols & Clark, L.L.C., No. 98 C 4280, 1999 WL)Tj
T*(284788, at *7 \(N.D. Ill. Apr. 26, 1999\))Tj
T*(\(citations omitted\).)Tj
ET

