T*()Tj
T*(/8 We point out that failure to object prior to)Tj
T*(confirmation does not foreclose a debtor from)Tj
T*(challenging fraudulent proofs of claim in the)Tj
T*(bankruptcy court. A bankruptcy confirmation may)Tj
T*(be revoked by the bankruptcy court if it was)Tj
T*(procured by fraud. See 11 U.S.C. sec. 1330\(a\).)Tj
T*(Further, parties who commit fraud on the)Tj
T*(bankruptcy court may be sanctioned by that court)Tj
T*(pursuant to Federal Rule of Bankruptcy Procedure)Tj
T*(9011, which is analogous to Rule 11 of the)Tj
T*(Federal Rules of Civil Procedure. See In re)Tj
T*(Bryson, 131 F.3d 601, 603 \(7th Cir. 1997\). Rule)Tj
T*(9011\(b\) explicitly requires all filings with the)Tj
T*(court to present only facts which the party)Tj
T*(reasonably believes to have evidentiary support;)Tj
T*(debtors facing fraudulent proofs of claim could)Tj
T*(seek sanctions under that section. A motion for)Tj
T*(sanctions under Rule 9011 must be made separately)Tj
T*(from the objection to a proof of claim,)Tj
T*(potentially giving debtors additional time to)Tj
T*(discover any fraud on the part of the creditor.)Tj
T*(See Fed. R. Bankr. P. 9011\(c\)\(1\)\(A\); In re Knox,)Tj
T*(237 B.R. 687, 698-99 \(Bankr. N.D. Ill. 1999\).)Tj
T*(Bankruptcy courts also have the authority to)Tj
T*(sanction attorneys under 28 U.S.C. sec. 1927,)Tj
T*(which allows the court to hold attorneys liable)Tj
T*(for any excess expenses caused because of their)Tj
T*(unreasonable or vexatious conduct. See In re)Tj
T*(Volpert, 110 F.3d 494, 500-01 \(7th Cir. 1997\).)Tj
T*()Tj
T*(/9 The FDCPA provides a definition of "debt":)Tj
T*()Tj
T*(The term "debt" means any obligation or alleged)Tj
T*(obligation of a consumer to pay money arising out)Tj
T*(of a transaction in which the money, property,)Tj
T*(insurance, or services which are the subject of)Tj
T*(the transaction are primarily for personal,)Tj
T*(family, or household purposes, whether or not)Tj
T*(such obligation has been reduced to judgment.)Tj
T*()Tj
T*(15 U.S.C. sec. 1692a\(5\).)Tj
T*()Tj
T*(/10 Because the parties have not presented the issue,)Tj
T*(we express no opinion as to whether a FDCPA claim)Tj
T*(can ever be predicated on a previous filing in a)Tj
T*(bankruptcy proceeding. Cf. Kokoszka v. Belford,)Tj
T*(417 U.S. 642, 650-52 \(1974\) \(holding that the)Tj
T*(wage garnishment protections of the Consumer)Tj
T*(Credit Protection Act sought to prevent persons)Tj
T*(from entering bankruptcy in the first place and)Tj
T*(therefore were not applicable when bankruptcy did)Tj
T*(occur\).)Tj
